  "worked_solution": "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{\u2033}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+15x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+49{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+15\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+49{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+15\\lambda +49] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+15\\lambda +49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+14\\lambda +49 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +7)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =-7.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-7}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-7}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.\n$$",
